# Naming — Skeleton

## 1. Applies when
Any project that names things. Universal layer: every genre enables this document.

## 2. This document owns
- Final names of all named entities (characters, factions, places, items, systems, UI terms).
- Placeholder management: the active placeholder registry (`{{double-brace}}` style), priority classes, and replacement status.
- Naming rationale (why a name works, its in-world logic, pronunciation/reading notes).
- The search-and-replace mapping table used when a placeholder gets its final name.
- Naming red lines: what naming must never decide (world facts).

## 3. This document does NOT own
- The facts behind a name (world truth lives in the Narrative Bible / World Design).
- Mechanics, stats or drop rates attached to a named thing (Gameplay / Resource).
- Dialogue lines that merely use a name (Script).

## 4. Recommended chapter structure
1. Document charter / 2. Name registry & status / 3. Characters / 4. Factions / 5. Places / 6. Technology & equipment / 7. Missions & events / 8. Items & collectibles / 9. UI & system terms / 10. Settled-term registry / 11. Search-and-replace mapping / 12. Open naming queue

## 5. Common boundaries
- Naming vs Bible: Naming decides *what it is called and why*; Bible decides *what it is*.
- Placeholder replacement is a batch operation driven by the mapping table — no single-document renames.

## 6. Common audit rules
- Every active placeholder appears exactly once in the registry with a status.
- A settled name must not still appear as `{{placeholder}}` anywhere (deprecated-placeholder sweep).
- Real-world name policy (dual-track fictional/real names) is declared once here, not per document.
